course: dictionary
lesson: 88
title: Ad, short for advertisement
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An ad is short for advertisement. It is a picture, video, poster or message that tries to get people to buy something or do something. You will see ads on TV, on YouTube, on buses and on giant billboards.
explain: Context helps you spot an ad. «Videos that are paid adverts are often marked "ad" or "sponsored", sometimes in small print.» So if you see the word ad, sponsored or a small label, ask yourself: is someone paying for me to watch this?
explain: A small ad is different. It is a short notice in a newspaper or online to sell something, or to find a job, a home or a lost pet. Words around it, such as "for sale" or "lost", are the clues.
fact: Ad is short for advertisement.
fact: «Ads sell feelings: happy, cool, grown-up.» An ad may make you want something because it makes you feel good, not because you need it.
fact: Ads often use famous footballers, cartoon animals, free toys and camera tricks.
fact: A small ad, also called a classified ad, is a short notice to sell something or find a job, a home or a lost pet.
fact: Some ads are marked "ad" or "sponsored", sometimes in small print, to show that someone paid for them.
exercise: What is an ad short for? | *Advertisement | Adventure | Address | Addition
exercise: «Videos that are paid adverts are often marked "ad" or "sponsored", sometimes in small print.» What does that small print tell you? | The video is only for babies | *Someone may have paid for the video to be shown | The video is a true story from history | The video has no sound
exercise: «Ads sell feelings: happy, cool, grown-up.» What do ads try to make you feel? | Nothing at all | Sleepy and bored | *Happy, cool or grown-up, so you want the thing | Scared of the dark
test: Where would you most likely find a small ad? | On the surface of the moon | *In a newspaper or online, to sell something | Inside a fridge | Inside a sealed envelope from the bank
test: Which trick can make a 10cm toy look 50cm tall in an ad? | *Camera tricks | Singing in the rain | Whispering | Reading a book aloud
exam: An ad says "9 out of 10 kids love it" but does not say which kids. Why should you ask which kids? | Because ads never use numbers | Because numbers in ads are always wrong | *The number may not tell the whole truth | Because all kids love everything
